  "raw_response": "I need to find the pattern that transforms the input grid to the output grid.\n\nLooking at the examples, I notice that the grids appear to have symmetry. Let me analyze more carefully.\n\nIn Example 1, comparing input and output:\n- The input has regions with value \"2\" that seem to form some pattern\n- In the output, those \"2\" values are replaced with values that would make the grid symmetric\n\nLet me check if the output is always symmetric in some way. Looking at Example 1, row 11 in input has `...6 2 2 4 7 7...` but output has `...6 9 6 4 7 7...`. The \"2\"s are replaced.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: The input grids have \"2\"s that mark regions that need to be filled in to create a symmetric pattern. The output appears to be horizontally and vertically symmetric (point symmetric or mirror symmetric).\n\nLet me check: In Example 1, the output row 11 is `1 8 9 9 7 7 4 6 9 6 7 1 1 7 6 9 6 4 7 7 9 9 8 1`. This is a palindrome! (reads same forwards and backwards)\n\nAnd comparing to the corresponding position from the other side: row 12 is identical to row 11 (since it's even index from bottom). Let me check vertical symmetry too.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: The grids appear to have 180-degree rotational symmetry (point symmetry) in the output.\n\nLet me verify with Example 1, output row 0 and row 23 (last):\n- Row 0: `6 9 6 4 4 5 4 9 9 8 7 1 1 7 8 9 9 4 5 4 4 6 9 6`\n- Row 23: `6 9 6 4 4 5 4 9 9 8 7 1 1 7 8 9 9 4 5 4 4 6 9 6` - Same!\n\nAnd row 1 vs row 22:\n- Row 1: `9 7 6 6 7 7 9 8 9 4 9 8 8 9 4 9 8 9 7 7 6 6 7 9`\n- Row 22: `9 7 6 6 7 7 9 8 9 4 9 8 8 9 4 9 8 9 7 7 6 6 7 9` - Same!\n\nSo the output has 180-degree rotational symmetry, meaning output[i][j] = output[23-i][23-j].\n\nAlso checking horizontal: Row 0 reversed is `6 9 6 4 4 5 4 9 9 8 7 1 1 7 8 9 9 4 5 4 4 6 9 6` which equals row 0. So it's also horizontally symmetric!\n\nThe rule seems to be: The output is both horizontally and vertically symmetric (180-degree rotational symmetry), and the \"2\" regions in the input are filled in to create this symmetry.\n\nMore specifically, for each cell, if it's not \"2\", keep it. If it is \"2\", fill it with the value from the corresponding symmetric position.\n\nFor 180-degree rotational symmetry: position (i,j) should equal position (23-i, 23-j).\n\nLet me apply this to the test input. For each \"2\" in the input, I'll replace it with the value that creates 180-degree rotational symmetry.\n\nActually, let me verify: in Example 1, input[11][15] = 2, output[11][15] = 9. Check: position (11,15) maps to (12,8) by 180-rotation. Input[12][8] = 6, but that's not 9...\n\nLet me check: 23-11=12, 23-15=8. So (11,15) \u2194 (12,8). Input[12][8] = 6, but output[11][15]=9.\n\nWait, let me re-read: output row 11 col 15 is 9? Let me check: `...6 9 6 4 7 7...` at positions 15,16,17,18,19: 6,9,6,4,7,7. So position 15 is 6, not 9.\n\nHmm let me recount: `1 8 9 9 7 7 4 6 9 6 7 1 1 7 6 9 6 4 7 7 9 9 8 1`\nIndices 0-23: 0=1,1=8,2=9,3=9,4=7,5=7,6=4,7=6,8=9,9=6,10=7,11=1,12=1,13=7,14=6,15=9,16=6,17=4,18=7,19=7,20=9,21=9,22=8,23=1\n\nSo position 15 is 9. And (12, 8) in input is 6. But output[12][8] should equal output[11][15]=9? Let me check output[12][8]: `1 8 9 9 7 7 4 6 9 6...` position 8 is 9. Yes!\n\nSo the rule is: output[i][j] = output[23-i][23-j], and we resolve conflicts by... actually looking at non-2 values.\n\nFor cells where input[i][j] != 2, we keep input[i][j]. For cells where input[i][j] == 2, we need to infer from symmetry.\n\nActually, looking at Example 1 row 11: input has `6 2 2 4 7 7` at positions 14-19, output has `6 9 6 4 7 7`. The \"2 2\" became \"9 6\". And position (12, 8-9) = (9, 6) which matches \"9 6\" reversed!\n\nSo for position (11,15)=2, we look at (12,8) which is 9 in output. And (12,8) in input is 6, not 2, so we can use it? No wait, input[12][8]=6, but output[12][8]=9.\n\nHmm, let me re-check. Input row 12: `1 8 9 9 7 7 4 6 2 2 2 1 1 7 6 2 2 4 2 2 2 9 8 1`. Position 8 is 2,